.TH CCOL_DYNAMIC_QUEUE_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs \- create a dynamic queue with no size limit and with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_dynamic_queue *ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs(
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a queue with no size limit, held in a
doubly-linked list. A send never waits for free space; instead it fails
in three cases: an allocation fails, the queue already holds
ccol_max_elem_count messages, or a ccol_dynmq_disable_sending(3) call
turned sends off. A new queue starts with sends turned on.

Every allocation of the queue, at creation time and for each list node
that a later send adds, goes through mmgmt_procs. The queue keeps its
own copy of those procedures and frees itself through them, so the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of the caller does not need to live longer than
this call. A NULL value selects the default malloc and free, which is
what the ccol_dynamic_queue_create() macro does.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a pointer to the new dynamic queue. If
err_str is not NULL, the function sets *err_str to NULL.

On failure, the function gives NULL, and if err_str is not NULL,
*err_str describes the reason. The reasons for a failure are: mmgmt_procs
is malformed, because it sets some but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc
and free; an allocation failed; the function could not initialize the
mutex of the queue or its condition variable; or, in a build that has
fork-safety protection compiled in, the function could not register the
mutex of the queue with the fork-safety registry. That registration also
fails once the process-exit destructors of the library have run and the
last queue is gone, for example in an application destructor that runs
after them. None of these paths leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
A send never blocks; instead of a wait, it fails in three cases: the
queue holds ccol_max_elem_count messages, the memory runs out, or sends
on the queue are turned off.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue: call
fork(2) before you create the queue, or create the queue, then call
fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One pattern is not supported: a
program that continues to run with no exec(3) in between while a queue
from before the fork is live. On top of that rule, the library adds a
defense in depth: the child never inherits a lock in the locked state
from another thread that held it at the instant of the fork.  Because
the queue also owns no thread of its own, a queue that a child inherits
in that way stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
